    body: `
<h3>The completed past</h3>
<p>English "came", "went", "ate" are for finished events. Hindi uses the completed past (the perfective): the stem plus -ā, -e, -ī or -ī̃, agreeing like an adjective. There is no <b>है</b> after it.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Masculine</th><th>Feminine</th></tr><tr><td>one</td><td class="k">आया</td><td class="k">आई</td></tr><tr><td>several, and आप</td><td class="k">आए</td><td class="k">आईं</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:वह कल आया।|vah kal āyā.|He came yesterday.}}
{{ex:वह कल आई।|vah kal āī.|She came yesterday.}}
<p>A few common verbs are irregular: <b>जाना</b> → <b>गया</b> <i>gayā</i>, <b>करना</b> → <b>किया</b> <i>kiyā</i>, <b>लेना</b> → <b>लिया</b>, <b>देना</b> → <b>दिया</b>, <b>पीना</b> → <b>पिया</b>.</p>
{{ex:हम कल जयपुर गए।|ham kal jaypur gae.|We went to Jaipur yesterday.}}
<h3>ने: when the verb takes an object</h3>
<p>Here Hindi does something English never does. With a verb that takes an object (eat something, see something, read something), the subject gets <b>ने</b> <i>ne</i>, a postposition. Pronouns join it and change form: <b>मैंने</b>, <b>आपने</b>, <b>उसने</b>, <b>हमने</b>, <b>उन्होंने</b> <i>unhõne</i>. Names stay as they are: <b>सारा ने</b>.</p>
<p>These forms look a little like the को forms you know (<b>मुझे</b>, <b>उसे</b>), but they are different words: <b>मैंने</b>, never <b>मुझने</b>.</p>
<p>Then the verb stops agreeing with the subject and agrees with the object instead.</p>
{{ex:मैंने ताज महल देखा।|maĩne tāj mahal dekhā.|I saw the Taj Mahal.}}
{{ex:मैंने चाय पी।|maĩne chāy pī.|I drank tea.}}
{{ex:सारा ने किताब पढ़ी।|sārā ne kitāb paṛhī.|Sarah read the book.}}
{{ex:मैंने दो समोसे खाए।|maĩne do samose khāe.|I ate two samosas.}}
<p><b>चाय</b> and <b>किताब</b> are feminine, so the verb ends in -ī, whoever drank or read. <b>ताज महल</b> is masculine, so <b>देखा</b>. Two samosas are plural, so <b>खाए</b>. A man and a woman say exactly the same thing.</p>
<h3>No ने with going and coming</h3>
<p>Verbs with no object, like <b>जाना</b>, <b>आना</b> and <b>सोना</b> (to sleep), never take <b>ने</b>. The verb agrees with the subject, as usual.</p>
{{ex:मैं दिल्ली गई।|maĩ dillī gaī.|I went to Delhi. (a woman speaking)}}
<p>Ask yourself: can I add "something" after the English verb? "I ate something" works, so <b>ने</b>. "I went something" doesn't, so no <b>ने</b>. A few verbs break the test: <b>लाना</b> (to bring) and <b>भूलना</b> (to forget) never take <b>ने</b>.</p>
<h3>आप in the past</h3>
<p>Without <b>ने</b>, the verb agrees with <b>आप</b> in the plural, as always: <b>आप कब आए?</b> to a man, <b>आप कब आईं?</b> to a woman. With <b>ने</b>, <b>आपने</b> changes nothing: the verb still follows the object.</p>
{{ex:आप कब आए?|āp kab āe?|When did you arrive? (to a man)}}
<h3>Questions and no</h3>
<p><b>आपने क्या किया?</b> means "What did you do?". For no, put <b>नहीं</b> before the verb: <b>मैंने खाना नहीं खाया</b>.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> forgetting <b>ने</b>, or agreeing with yourself after it. <b>मैं चाय पिया</b> → <b>मैंने चाय पी</b>.</div>
`,
