    body: `
<p>Good news: Hindi joins sentences much as English does. The joining word goes between the two parts, and each part keeps its own verb at the end.</p>
<h3>और, लेकिन, या</h3>
<p><b>और</b> <i>aur</i> is "and", <b>लेकिन</b> <i>lekin</i> is "but", and <b>या</b> <i>yā</i> is "or". In everyday speech <b>पर</b> also means "but". You know <b>पर</b> as the postposition "on"; when it means "but", it stands between two sentences.</p>
{{ex:मुझे चाय पसंद है, और कॉफ़ी भी।|mujhe chāy pasand hai, aur kŏfī bhī.|I like tea, and coffee too.}}
{{ex:चाय या कॉफ़ी?|chāy yā kŏfī?|Tea or coffee?}}
{{ex:मैं थका हुआ हूँ, लेकिन ख़ुश हूँ।|maĩ thakā huā hū̃, lekin ḵhush hū̃.|I'm tired but happy. (a man speaking)}}
<p><b>लेकिन</b> is safe in any situation. <b>पर</b> sounds more casual. Both can join a short afterthought too, as in English "I'll come, but late".</p>
{{ex:मैं आऊँगा, पर देर से।|maĩ āū̃gā, par der se.|I'll come, but late. (a man speaking)}}
<p><b>और</b> joins two nouns (<b>चाय और कॉफ़ी</b>) or two whole sentences. Between sentences it simply adds one idea to another.</p>
<h3>Because and so</h3>
<p>These two point in opposite directions, and English speakers often swap them.</p>
<ul><li><b>क्योंकि</b> <i>kyõki</i> ("because") introduces the cause. The <b>क्योंकि</b> part usually comes after the main sentence, as in English.</li><li><b>इसलिए</b> <i>islie</i> ("so, that's why") introduces the result. The cause comes first.</li></ul>
{{ex:मैं आज घर पर हूँ क्योंकि मुझे बुख़ार है।|maĩ āj ghar par hū̃ kyõki mujhe buḵhār hai.|I'm at home today because I have a fever.}}
{{ex:मुझे बुख़ार है, इसलिए मैं घर पर हूँ।|mujhe buḵhār hai, islie maĩ ghar par hū̃.|I have a fever, so I'm at home.}}
<p>Both sentences say the same thing. The fever always goes with <b>क्योंकि</b> or before <b>इसलिए</b>.</p>
<p>When someone asks <b>क्यों?</b> ("why?"), the answer usually starts with <b>क्योंकि</b>, just as an English answer starts with "because".</p>
<h3>Then: फिर</h3>
<p>For one event after another, Hindi uses <b>फिर</b> <i>phir</i>, "then". You can also say <b>और फिर</b>, "and then".</p>
{{ex:पहले हम आगरा गए, फिर जयपुर।|pahle ham āgrā gae, phir jaypur.|First we went to Agra, then to Jaipur.}}
<h3>तो: so, well then</h3>
<p><b>तो</b> <i>to</i> starts a sentence that follows from what was just said, like English "so" or "well then".</p>
{{ex:अच्छा, तो कल मिलेंगे।|acchhā, to kal milẽge.|OK, so we'll meet tomorrow.}}
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Hindi</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">और</td><td>and</td></tr><tr><td class="k">लेकिन, पर</td><td>but</td></tr><tr><td class="k">या</td><td>or</td></tr><tr><td class="k">क्योंकि</td><td>because (cause)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">इसलिए</td><td>so, that's why (result)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">फिर</td><td>then, after that</td></tr><tr><td class="k">तो</td><td>so, well then</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Inside each part, everything you have learned still applies: the verb agrees and comes last.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using <b>क्योंकि</b> for "so". <b>मुझे बुख़ार है, क्योंकि मैं घर पर हूँ</b> → <b>मुझे बुख़ार है, इसलिए मैं घर पर हूँ</b>.</div>
`,
